============================================================
FEATURE–SEPARATING FRAMEWORK
============================================================
UNIVERSAL 
------------------------------------------------------------
The invariant ν_F(X) is:

  - A teaching dimension for singletons under feature family F
  - A minimal discriminating set in the Boolean algebra
    generated by F
  - A constrained binary coding length for X
  - A measure of the expressive power of F on X
  - A categorical embedding dimension into a Boolean cube

1. BASIC OBJECTS
----------------
Let X be any finite set.
Let F ⊆ {0,1}^X be any family of Boolean predicates on X.

Each predicate φ ∈ F is a map:
    φ : X → {0,1}

Think of F as a "feature family" available for distinguishing
elements of X.

------------------------------------------------------------
2. FEATURE EVALUATION FUNCTOR
------------------------------------------------------------
Given any subfamily Q ⊆ F, define the evaluation map:
    v_Q : X → {0,1}^|Q|
    v_Q(x) = ( φ(x) )_{φ ∈ Q}

This is a functor from the discrete category on X to the
Boolean cube, with coordinates drawn from Q.

------------------------------------------------------------
3. SEPARATING SUBFAMILIES
------------------------------------------------------------
A subfamily Q ⊆ F is *separating* for X when:
    v_Q is injective on X

Equivalently:
    For all distinct x,y ∈ X,
    there exists φ ∈ Q such that φ(x) ≠ φ(y).

------------------------------------------------------------
4. FEATURE-SEPARATING NUMBER
------------------------------------------------------------
Define:
    ν_F(X) = min{ |Q| : Q ⊆ F and Q separates X }

This is the minimal number of features from F needed to
uniquely encode every element of X.

------------------------------------------------------------
5. INFORMATION-THEORETIC LOWER BOUND
------------------------------------------------------------
Any injective map X → {0,1}^m requires:
    m ≥ ceil(log2 |X|)

Thus:
    ν_F(X) ≥ ceil(log2 |X|)

The gap:
    Δ_F(X) = ν_F(X) - ceil(log2 |X|)
measures how restrictive the feature family F is.

------------------------------------------------------------
6. SPECIALIZATION TO GROUPS
------------------------------------------------------------
Let C be any finite class of finite groups.

Examples of feature families F:

  - Prime-power divisibility:
        F = { Q_{p^k} : p^k divides |G| }
  - Element-order predicates:
        F = { 1_{d | ord(g)} }
  - Conjugacy-class size predicates
  - Sylow-number predicates
  - Character-degree predicates
  - Fixed-point-ratio predicates
  - Any Boolean-valued invariant family

Then:
    ν_F(C)
is the minimal number of such features needed to distinguish
all groups in C.

------------------------------------------------------------


